1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,505
What can AI really tell us about

2
00:00:02,505 --> 00:00:03,391
a brain MRI?

3
00:00:04,323 --> 00:00:05,595
This is BrainLens,

4
00:00:05,891 --> 00:00:08,050
a research website built to make scan

5
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:09,914
results easier to explore.

6
00:00:10,323 --> 00:00:12,982
Upload a supported MRI slice and review

7
00:00:12,982 --> 00:00:14,709
the suggested tumour category.

8
00:00:15,368 --> 00:00:16,618
In this saved example,

9
00:00:16,868 --> 00:00:19,050
the dataset label is meningioma.

10
00:00:19,505 --> 00:00:22,073
BrainLens also suggests meningioma.

11
00:00:22,436 --> 00:00:23,845
That's a match for this image,

12
00:00:24,118 --> 00:00:26,232
not proof that every prediction will be

13
00:00:26,232 --> 00:00:29,232
correct. For compatible three-dimensional scans,

14
00:00:29,232 --> 00:00:30,982
explore different views,

15
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,982
inspect candidate regions and check their measured

16
00:00:33,982 --> 00:00:37,436
volumes. The core research workflows are built.

17
00:00:37,936 --> 00:00:41,527
Experimental two-dimensional tumour outlines still need improvement,

18
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:43,164
so they remain offline.

19
00:00:43,755 --> 00:00:46,141
Category suggestions are available separately.

20
00:00:46,777 --> 00:00:49,118
These suggestions aren't confirmed diagnoses,

21
00:00:49,936 --> 00:00:52,277
and a negative result doesn't prove a

22
00:00:52,277 --> 00:00:55,232
healthy brain. Our next milestone is independent

23
00:00:55,232 --> 00:00:58,777
evaluation using properly separated patient data.

24
00:00:58,777 --> 00:01:01,982
I'm sharing BrainLens to invite feedback and

25
00:01:01,982 --> 00:01:03,686
help make the next version better.
